= Noske Moves on the revolutonary militias.

<br> The recent November Revolution has seen many organised militias, commanded by impromptu soldier’s soviets, rise up against the Kaiser in the wake of the Kiel Mutiny.

This included the Volksmarinedivision (People’s Marine Division) made up of left-wing naval marines, Republikanischer Soldatenwehr organised by Otto Wels and the KPD-aligned Red Militias. However, despite their pivotal role in conducting the revolution, tensions quickly flared between the Militias (And thus Soldier Soviets) and the Republican Provisional Government, namely the 1918 Christmas crisis (Weihnachtskämpfe) where Otto Wels was kidnapped by the Volksmarinedivision after a dispute about pay. This had significantly weakened the ties between the Council of People's Deputies and Provisional Government, leading to the Spartacist Uprising in January, with many of the Red Militias suppressed by the Freikorps, which was given near total control over the security situation by the Minister of the Reichswehr, Gustav Noske and interim Chairman Freidrich Ebert. 

However, with the suppression of the Spartacist Uprising and the dissolution of the Council of People's Deputies last month, Noske and Ebert now consider the militias as obsolete and arms of the KPD, and are pushing for their immediate disbandment.

many of the militias and the German Workers' and Soldiers' Soviet that was formed during the Revolution feel betrayed due to this, as they feel the Provisional Government has failed to fulfill their promises made to the Soviet during the Revolution.

This has only worsened in the wake of the Spartacist Uprising, and the empowerment of the Freikorps during the brief crisis. This has led to minor street fighting throughout February as the various militias and Freikorps clash in the streets across Germany, and now the Soviet has grown impatient with the Provisional Government’s backsliding on their promises.

On the 3rd of March, the Greater Berlin Council Workers’ and Soldiers Soviets voted to launch an immediate general strike to pressure the government into accepting their demands. These include the immediate restructuring of the Reichswehr to be aligned with the democratic government, an immediate dissolving of the Freikorps, reform of the administration and purges of Imperial-Era officials and reactionary cadres, and the immediate fulfilment of the various promises of socialisation promised to them in the aftermath of the Revolution.

This has been met with an armed response by the government, with the Prussian State Ministry declaring a state of emergency (Ausnahmezustand) over Berlin, with Noske appointed as "Commander In Chief of the Marches" to supposedly deal with the "Communist Insurgency". Already government troops have trashed the offices of "Die Rote Fahrne", and fighting is only expected to increase in the coming days as the militias and Freikorps face off in the streets. A cabinet meeting has been scheduled to discuss the Cabinet’s response to the General Strike and devise a response...

- @diplomacy_triumph
- @the_ends_justify_the_means

@diplomacy_triumph
title: Perhaps a more diplomatic course can be taken?
choose-if: spd_relation >= 65 and linksliberale_strength + pazifisten_strength > nationalliberale_strength and coalition_dissent <= 1
unavailable_subtitle: Noske has too much influence within the Cabinet...
on-arrival: spd_relation += 10; uspd_relation += 8; kpd_relation += 6; pro-republic += 7; soldatenwehr_available = 1; spd_workers += 8; uspd_radicalisation -= 2; ddp_workers += 8; new_middle_ddp += 4; old_middle_ddp -= 5; dnvp_relation -= 8; dvp_relation -= 5
  
<img src="img/noske_failure.png" style="float:right; margin-left:1em; margin-bottom:0.5em; object-fit:cover; object-position:top; border:5px solid #000000ff;"> At the cabinet meeting on the 4th of March, our Ministers have argued against Noske’s quick deployment of the Freikorps, calling it undemocratic and an inappropriate and unproportional response to a mere strike. We were quickly joined by Rudolf Wissel, and despite Noske’s vocal objections towards negotiations with "Bolshevik agitators", Schiedermann’s own doubts about Noske’s plan and Wissel’s backing has caused Noske’s plan for a violent crushing of the strike to be shelved. To secure government control of Berlin, the Government has begun immediate negotiations with the USPD and Strike Committee to come to an amenable deal on the socialisation plan, causing much of the strike’s momentum and violence to fizzle out. 
  
Limited streetfighting by KPD radicals has broken out on the streets of Berlin, leading to a deployment of the Freikorps in the most violent districts being authorised.  By the 8th, the remaining Red Militias were violently broken up by the Freikorps, while the bulk of the Republikanischer Soldatenwehr has been left alone on the Government’s insistence. They have also gained many members of the now disbanded Volksmarinedivision, who have injected the Soldatenwehr with a boost of experienced soldiers and arms, increasing their strength. 

The General Strike has been called off as talks continue between the Government and Soviets, and this has boosted the support for the Republic on the whole and once more pulled together the fraying workers movement. Leo Jogiches was unfortunately assassinated by non-government sponsored right-wing militants during the fighting however, and thus Paul Levi has been elevated to serve as leader in the interim. The “Law for the Provisional Reichswehr” which was due to be proposed has also been amended following Wissel and our objections, with the passage allowing internal use requiring explicit cabinet authorization and approval from the Interior Ministry which we control. Internally, this has caused widespread relief amongst the party as many see this as the beginning of the end of the widespread revolutionary violence, however some National Liberals are disappointed at our support for the “Revolutionary Bolshevik Militias”.

@the_ends_justify_the_means
title: Unfortunately, we must restore order with whatever means necessary…
on-arrival: pro-republic -= 7; nationalism += 5; spd_relation -= 5; uspd_radicalisation += 2; uspd_relation -= 10; kpd_relation -= 10; spd_workers -= 10; uspd_workers += 7; kpd_workers += 3; ddp_workers -= 8;

<img src="img/noske_triumph.png" style="float:right; margin-left:1em; margin-bottom:0.5em; object-fit:cover; object-position:top; border:5px solid #000000ff;"> With no major objections at the Cabinet , Noske has fully deployed the Reichswehr into Berlin to put down the supposed “Uprising” by the militias. The first confrontation has led to an immediate clash between the Volksmarinendivision and Guard’s Cavalry Rifle Divison, with heavy streetfighting between the Volksmarinendivision, Red Militias and Republikanischer Soldatenwehr against Freikorps has left parts of Berlin destroyed, and hundreds dead. By the 8th, the General Strike was ended by MSPD-aligned members in the Soviets, however streetfighting continued until the 13th. Meanwhile, the Weimar National Assembly has also voted to adopt the "Law on the Provisonal Reichswehr", making the Freikorps a defacto police force in suppressing the radical left.

Controversially, following reports on the 8th about members of the the Volksmarinendivision storming the Lichtenberg police headquarters, a false rumor about the supposed summary executions of 60 officials led to the issuing of an illegal summary execution order by Noske to government forces, ordering the execution of any person carrying a firearm by government troops.
  
The next day, Leo Jogiches was shot after being arrested by government forces, and on the 11th the Marloh Massacre occurred, where 30 actively disbanding members of the Volksmarinendivision who had taken amnesty in exchange for their backpay had been lined up and machinegunned by members of the Freikorps. This has significantly damaged our relations with the USPD and KPD, along with forcing the immediate disbanding of the Soviet, Volksmarinendivision, Republikanischer Soldatenwehr and Red Militias. The MSPD’s support among the workers has further deteriorated, and the left has divided amongst themselves even more.

Does the end truly justify the means?
